1. She is a British VTuber. (Verbatim: "Hi, I'm Elias Delaria. I'm British VTuber. Nice to meet you.")
2. She was ill for about a week, which was longer than her usual recovery time. (Verbatim: "I've been ill for like the last week and I think the reason why I was ill for a bit longer than what I think would be my usual recovery time...")
3. She owns a Samoyed dog named Lila. (Verbatim: "my dog, Lila, being the annoying dog that she is" and "a Samoyed, like big white...")
4. Her dog Lila is attention-seeking and jealous of other dogs. (Verbatim: "My dog is in the other room because she's annoying as heck and a jealous little bum who won't leave this dog alone.")
5. She has experience dog sitting, including for a dog afraid of fireworks. (Verbatim: "I'm currently dog sitting... this dog is very afraid of fireworks.")
6. She lives in England, where Bonfire Night (Guy Fawkes Night) is celebrated on November 5th. (Verbatim: "And in England, we have something called Bonfire Night... Guy Fawkes is on the 5th.")
7. She recorded a voice pack for Halloween, despite being ill. (Verbatim: "for halloween we've released a voice pack... i was still 12 hours late of that deadline...")
8. She missed her PC and felt bored while sick in bed. (Verbatim: "i missed my pc and yeah like the bad twitter space you don't need to break my phone right...")
9. She intended to record singing but was too ill to do so. (Verbatim: "i supposed to record some like singing and that did not happen")
10. She walks multiple dogs, including her Samoyed and a mixed-breed brown dog. (Verbatim: "I'm walking these two dogs, okay, one's a Samoyed and one's this dog, which is, what are you? I don't know what it is...") [end of text]



1. She likes chicken burgers, especially McDonald's and her own homemade version. (Verbatim: "okay you see why i really like chicken burgers right because because because the mcdonald's chicken burgers just so heck and yummy... mine is better mine is a homemade chicken burger")

2. She has never tried a proper American burger but wants to try one someday. (Verbatim: "i've heard legends of these american burgers i need to try one i need to try one one day")

3. She finds large burgers unhealthy and physically uncomfortable to eat. (Verbatim: "i can only handle the little burger like anymore my heart gets upset it's like oh gosh this is bad for me")

4. She has not seen a Wendy's in England and believes American fast-food chains like Taco Bell and Whataburger are rare there. (Verbatim: "i've personally not seen a wendy's in england i think we do have them i just don't know where")

5. She is afraid of London and would not want to live there due to it being too busy, scary, and expensive. (Verbatim: "london scares me i don't think i could live in london i'll be honest it's too busy and scary and expensive")

6. She has stayed in a rough area of London and had a negative experience, including seeing people eating out of bins. (Verbatim: "i stayed in somewhere very rough and there were people eating out of the bins um where i stayed and i was like i'm understanding the price now")

7. She has traveled to Japan and spent about a month there, comparing its train system favorably to London's. (Verbatim: "i was in japan for for like a month and yeah it was just so different")

8. She appreciates the quietness and orderliness of Japanese public transport, especially compared to noisy, drunk passengers on English trains. (Verbatim: "it was just silent... in england... there's always always always at least one group of really loud drunks")

9. She finds the London Underground (tube) zone system complex but navigable by following colored lines. (Verbatim: "you just follow the colors... and then yeah that's it")

10. She used contactless payment on public transport in London and found the system convenient. (Verbatim: "you just use your contactless card and beep boop and never check how much they charged you") [end of text]



1. She is originally from England and considers it her home. (Verbatim: "england is home so when i came back from japan and obviously i landed in london because that's the only way to really get anywhere out of england flying wise")

2. She has lived in or visited Japan and experienced a cultural contrast upon returning to England. (Verbatim: "when i came back from japan and obviously i landed in london... i was like oh i'm right back home ah there's that sweet noise i'm used to")

3. She finds the loud and rowdy behavior of people in England, such as drunks shouting, familiar and oddly comforting despite it being typically undesirable. (Verbatim: "everybody's so rowdy and loud and annoying how lovely i feel like i'm at home again... ah there's that sweet noise i'm used to of the drunks shouting")

4. She is accustomed to trains in England being late and finds punctuality unusual. (Verbatim: "the train's not on time perfect that is exactly what i expected i'm not ready for it to be on time")

5. She experienced a stark contrast in customer service between Japan and England, noting Japanese service as very polite and formal, while English service was loud and rude. (Verbatim: "customer service in japan's very very very nice everyone's very polite... my first encounter with customer service is like the loudest most angry shouting lady ever at the passport machines")

6. She previously worked in retail in England, where she was required to approach customers and ask if they needed help, which she found uncomfortable. (Verbatim: "i used to have to be that retail person that went up to people and i just felt bad because like i personally would not want it")

7. She was once transferred to work in the men’s section of a store, including the shoe area, which made her uncomfortable. (Verbatim: "they were like oh ellia we need you to go to the men's section... i will go to the men's section for today yes it'll be fine... i'm gonna work on the men's shoes and do that")

8. She was followed by a group of teenagers while working, which caused her stress, and one was encouraged by peers to ask for her number. (Verbatim: "i was getting followed around by like a group of teenagers for like an hour... eventually i felt i felt like one of them got pushed by the others to like come up to me and like ask me for my number")

9. A coworker made a racist comment suggesting she was not asked for her number because she is mixed race, which deeply affected her. (Verbatim: "oh you know you didn't ask for your number because you're pretty it's just because you're mixed race and i was like what excuse me um that's racist to say")

10. She finds it extremely creepy and inappropriate when strangers introduce themselves by revealing they know her home address, and strongly advises against it. (Verbatim: "never never introduce yourself to somebody you've never spoken to by telling them that you know where they live it's just it's not it's not a good first line") [end of text]



1. She likes playing cute characters in games and embraces her preference despite stereotypes. (Verbatim: "I like cute things and no one's gonna stop me from liking cute things I just you know what it's just what I like.")

2. She intentionally chose ambiguous usernames to avoid gender assumptions, though her account remains distinctly feminine. (Verbatim: "I made sure I'd always try and set like my name to like something ambiguous so that like it wasn't too like oh my god I'm a girl like that kind of thing.")

3. She strongly dislikes boosting in games, believing it places players in ranks they don’t deserve and ruins the team experience. (Verbatim: "I don't want to be boosted... I'd rather be good at the game.")

4. She feels guilt when performing poorly in team-based games, believing it negatively impacts her teammates. (Verbatim: "I feel so guilty I'm literally like playing it and I'm like... I'm so sorry dear team for ruining your fun by just sucking so much.")

5. She attended secondary school career advice sessions, which she found unhelpful and dismissive, particularly when advised against athletic careers. (Verbatim: "She was just like yeah just do whatever just don't do sport and I was like any other advice?")

6. She chose to take Music and Drama for her GCSEs instead of expected academic subjects like Computing, to challenge her shyness and quiet nature. (Verbatim: "I picked music and drama which really shocked people because they were like no you're the quiet nerdy girl like what are you doing in a music class?")

7. She wanted to overcome her shyness and build confidence, which motivated her to take Drama and perform on stage despite initial fear. (Verbatim: "I wanted to learn how to not be so quiet not be so shy I wanted to push myself.")

8. She found performing on stage initially terrifying but eventually exciting and rewarding, especially after learning to focus on doing her best. (Verbatim: "I remember just feeling really it was really exciting it was really exciting it made my heart go ducky donkey when I was on the stage.")

9. She regrets briefly thinking she had closed off future opportunities in computing by not taking it at GCSE level, but later realized doors can reopen. (Verbatim: "I remember regretting my choice for a bit being like oh my god I've shut off all the doors to computing... but I managed you can you can reopen those doors.")

10. She holds a Master’s degree in Computing, despite not having a GCSE in the subject. (Verbatim: "I don't have a GCSE in computing but I have my masters in it.") [end of text]



1. She studied music and drama in university, which confused her parents. (Verbatim: "my parents were very confused why i picked music and drama i just didn't tell them at first")

2. She earned a bachelor’s degree and received her diploma in a formal ceremony. (Verbatim: "on the day of my when i finished my bachelor's okay like when i actually got the degree like got the paper")

3. Her parents initially wanted her to pursue science/medicine, which she studied partly to meet their expectations. (Verbatim: "because i think i think he knew that i just mostly did all this sciencey stuff um one because i was good at it and two because it was what they wanted")

4. She worked full-time as a software developer before becoming a VTuber. (Verbatim: "i was going through the whole like oh i'm gonna quit my full-time job as a developer and become a vtuber")

5. Her father expressed pride in her entertainment abilities and encouraged her not to give up on acting or performing. (Verbatim: "papa elia saying to me like like congratulating me gave me a hug and said that you know proud of me... told me not to give that up")

6. She struggled with parental pressure and conditional approval, feeling her achievements were attributed to her parents rather than her own effort. (Verbatim: "you only did so well because we gave you such talents and brains because you are our child")

7. She was frequently compared to her cousin Ellie during childhood, creating feelings of unfair competition. (Verbatim: "they'd be like yeah so any cousin's greatest this and and so what did ellia get")

8. Her cousin Ellie had access to private tutors, which she felt gave an unfair advantage over her. (Verbatim: "ellie cousin had the advantage of private tutors okay i was like yo this is not a fair race")

9. She values intrinsic motivation but was raised in an environment that emphasized extrinsic, competitive validation. (Verbatim: "i wish i was raised in more intrinsic motivators of you know yeah instead of everything's a competition in life")

10. She decided to become a VTuber after remembering her father’s encouragement to continue pursuing entertainment, helping her through an existential crisis. (Verbatim: "it helped me with i was going through such a existential crisis then... when i had to do the whole like oh i'm gonna quit my full-time job as a developer and become a vtuber") [end of text]



